## Decisions 

When I Started this project I had a major lack of direction as this is the first sort of project I have done;
I wanted to model some Weather data to see if I could quantify climate change in my local area;

-- Cleaning of Data and issues with decisions I made --
I began by downloading a data set from the met office and trying to clean it by identifying extra characters on values that I wanted to use and changing '---' to NANs.
I chose to use the maximum teprature data just to keep everything as simple as possible.
This is where I unkowingly made a dodgy decision; I figured due to two gaps of data in 1940, 41 and 44 it would be easier to remove these years entirely and carry on as if they
never happened. This was a bad idea as I inteded to compare my model with a SARIMA model that I would go on to fit but this clearly cannot be done as the number of observations
has a large impact on comparison of values such as: Log Likelihood, AIC, BIC.
To fix this you would use a Kalman filter to fill in values predicted by the model; This however took me away from finishing the actual project so I put it aside to do after I 
finished this project.

-- Using Classes to display Results --
Starting on the Main part of the Harmonic and Auto Regression code I was new and unfamiliar with using classes so my initial code included just the function itself.
To me this wasnt good enough as when looking at a model there are several values associated with it that you may want to access such as residuals and log likelihood etc.

-- Fitting AR on Harmonic regression --
During my first run over of my code i just fitted the Harmonic regression that handeled a linear trend term and harmonic terms, this wasnt bad but I wanted a closer fit.
So after having gone through fitting SARIMA already I thought it wouldnt be too hard to fit an AR model on my residuals hence I recoded Quenouilles test to give me 
the first lag that is significant. It is important to know that this method is vunerable to more complex processes but in general should be fine.

-- SARIMA Model -- 
As the Stats models module for SARIMAX has a Kalman filter built in i started by defining SARIMA_data as the raw data with no values dropped.
Then for simplicity, I preformed tests on the data with the years missing years removed to keep NANs from interfering and to stop issues with having strange ACF values.
This is reasonable as fill the NANs with predicted values you must already have a model Which I did not.

-- Determining if a Non Seasonal differencing term is necessary --
It is common practice to test using ADF and KPSS as they test both wether a unit root exists (series is non statonary) but from difference H0s;
ADF assumes a unit root exists whereas KPSS assumes Stationarity. Both for stationarity arround a mean and arround a linear trend gave me non stationary 
so it follows that there would be no term by term differencing required from my series.

-- Determining if there is a Seasonal differencing term reguired -- 
CH and OCSB tests do the same thing as KPSS and ADF (testing from a different hypothesis) with their main difference being that aside from testing for a seasonal unit root,
they cant cope with a linear trend present so prior to preforming the test I used a least squares regression to remove the linear trend;
Both tests resulted in D = 0 which was concerning for me as by this point I had already finished my code and was rewriting to get rid of any errors,
such as using certain series in testing that would lead to incorrect results. Previously I had got a contradiction and a further tested for a drop in variance
to conclude that differencing was neccecary. This was an invalid method in my current case as it relied on
a change in variance between a differenced and non differenced series which is not a meaninful test on wether a seasonal unit root exists. Testing the Variance like this is more 
justified to prevent over differencing. 

-- Going Forward with d = 0, D = 0 --
In my first run of coding where I had D = 1, the process when much more smoothly as I proceeded to test using Bartlett's approximation and further testing using Quenouille's test.
Whereas now I checked my Seasonal ACF and PACF values to see if there were any noticable cut off points to help inform me wether I had an MA/AR process.
After looking at their graphs it seemed to me that I could have an ARMA process as the ACF at seasonal lags never had a sharp cut off. Obviously performing Bartlett's test
on this data would lead to no significant conclusion as it directly depends on acf values.

-- Seasonal aplication of EACF --
To identify the paramaters of your ARMA process it is common to use an EACF "test"; 
which calculates acf values then fits an AR model order 1, calculates residuals then, calculates acf on thoes and continues this up to AR(p).
After recieving my result my confusion was furthered as there was in the True/False significance matrix there was no permenant row of true values past a certain point like you 
would expect to see. So given my past experience with having a easy time with further testing I tried it on the Differeced series which gave me the result I saw before.
AR(0), D = 1, MA(1).

-- Non seasonal testing --
Using the eacf test while setting the period = 1, the resulting matrix inferred that the process was AR(0), MA(3) so to check this I used Bartlett's then Quenouille's on the 
resulting residuals after fitting MA(2). Crucially Bartlett's test suggested that MA(2) was more correct and that after Quenouille's suggested AR(0) was correct. As these two
differences are relatively small I took a look at the final model with Both models fully fitted, and then looked at the BIC values and compared to see which was lower.
I looked at BIC instead of AIC as BIC as penalty term kln(n) where k is number of paramaters and n is number of observations; this is important as AIC just has penality term 2k
this does not depend on n hence rewards models for using more terms in my case n = 1743. With using BIC it rewards me for identifying key driving factors of the series.
Hence the final model is SARIMAX(0,0,1)(0,1,1,12) with a linear trend term added. The linear trend term is not entirely neccecary as it is very small I mainly wanted it for 
comparison purposes.


-- Why Bartlett's aprox and Quenouille's test --
Bartlett's aprox is good as aside from the initial aproximation itself after you spesify a spesific hypothesis the maths becomes simple meaning it is easy to code also
ACF suposedly has a sharp cut off after lag q making it easy to identify significant values for a True MA(q) process.
Quenouilles test is useful as like Bartlett's the maths is relativly simple making it easy to code and it gives useful information for an AR(p) process as each component 
it tests is independant as it uses Theoretical PACF values. Hence both tests were convenient for checking my results.










